---
import BlogPostImage from '../../../../../components/BlogPostImage.astro';
import BlogPostShowcaseVideo from '../../../../../components/BlogPostShowcaseVideo.astro';
import Heading from '../../../../../components/Heading.astro';
import BlogPost from '../../../../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---

<BlogPost
	title="Create a custom rick roll link using GitHub Pages"
	description="How to make your own rick roll link with GitHub Pages, so the link doesn't look suspicious and chat apps don't show a preview of the video."
	seoKeywords="rick roll, custom link, github pages, create custom rick roll link, doing the god's work"
	postReleaseDate="01-05-2023"
	postCategory="Tutorial"
	postIntroduction="We've all been there, one of your friends asked you for some link and you couldn't resist the urge to send them a rick roll, however nowadays Rickrolling is becoming increasingly harder, as some people can recognize the link nearly instantly and text messaging apps show previews of the video. We, as programmers, need to make information about creating unrecognizable rick rolls more widespread and contribute our part to the community 🫡."
>
	<Heading level="2" text="Showcase" />
	<BlogPostShowcaseVideo src="./showcase-rick-roll-working.webm" />

	<Heading level="2" text="Prerequisites" />
	<ul>
		<li>
			Have a Github account. If you don't have one, go ahead and
			<a
				href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-overview#scenarios"
				class="underline"
				target="_blank"
				rel="nofollow noreferrer noopener external"
			>
				set it up</a
			>.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<Heading level="2" text="Step by step guide" />
	<ol>
		<li>
			Ok, you're logged in and see the home page of
			<a href="https://github.com" class="underline" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noreferrer noopener external">github.com</a>. The first thing
			you need to do is click on your profile logo in the top right corner ("1" in the picture) of the page and click on select "Your
			repositories" in the pop-up menu ("2" in the picture).
			<BlogPostImage src="./first-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>

		<li>
			Now, on this page should be a green button that has "New" written on it ("3" in the picture). Go ahead and click on it.
			<BlogPostImage src="./second-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>

		<li>
			Now, you're creating a repository. Make sure that your settings are identical to the ones shown below. Especially the part where you make
			your repository "Public", otherwise it won't work (without Pro/Enterprise account). In the textbox repository name, write the name that
			you want your link to have. For example, I have "link-name", that means that my final link would look like this:
			https://tdiblik.github.io/link-name. This link is composed of: https://YOUR_USERNAME.github.io/REPOSITORY_NAME. After you're done with it
			you can go ahead and click on the "Create repository" button.
			<BlogPostImage src="./third-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>

		<li>
			After that you'll be redirected to another page, this page home page of your repository. Here, you need to click on "creating a new file"
			<BlogPostImage src="./forth-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>

		<li>
			That should redirect you to another page, where you need to make sure that you input the following information into the circled sections.
			To the first circled section, write: <code is:raw class="language-zsh inline">_config.yml</code>. And to the second circled section,
			write: <code is:raw class="language-zsh inline">theme: jekyll-theme-cayman</code>. After you've done that, continue by clicking the
			"Commit changes" button and then confirm it inside the pop-up.
			<BlogPostImage src="./fifth-step.png" width="1200px" />
			<BlogPostImage src="./sixth-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>

		<li>
			Once you've confirmed the commit, you will be once again redirected to the repository home page. From here, you want to create another new
			file called <code is:raw class="language-zsh inline">index.html</code>, insert the following code into it, and commit it (following
			pictures as instructions).
			<pre class="code-wrapper small"><code is:raw class="language-html">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;meta charset="utf-8" /&gt;
&lt;title>Oh no no no :D&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;meta
  http-equiv="refresh"
  content="0; URL=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ"
/&gt;</code></pre>
			<BlogPostImage src="./seventh-step.png" width="1200px" />
			<BlogPostImage src="./eight-step.png" width="1200px" />
			<BlogPostImage src="./nineth-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>

		<li>
			Right now, we need to activate the github pages page and we're all done. You can do that by going into Settings -> Pages -> Branch, then
			select master or main (depending on your settings) and click on "Save".
			<BlogPostImage src="./final-step.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>
		<li>
			All you need to do is wait for a minute and then try your link. You can either construct it yourself following the pattern
			https://YOUR_USERNAME.github.io/REPOSITORY_NAME, or you can see it in the same "Pages" settings page.
			<BlogPostImage src="./where-to-see-link.png" width="1200px" />
		</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		And that's it! If you're interested in learning how it works or want to use a different video instead of rick roll, you can read about it in
		the
		<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ" class="underline" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noreferrer noopener external"
			>next blog post</a
		>.
	</p>
</BlogPost>
